<h1>Note distance</h1>
<p class="lede">Every melody is a chain of distances: up a little, down a lot, back home. This page measures those distances in half steps and whole steps,
draws them so you can see them, and gives you exercises to sing them until you can feel them without counting.
Everything stays inside one octave — do re mi fa sol la ti do — the range almost any voice can sing.</p>

<h2>Your do</h2>
<p>Pick a do you can sing comfortably, with the do an octave above it still in reach. Every exercise on the page starts from it. Most men’s voices are comfortable with a do around C3 to F3; most women’s and children’s around A3 to C4. Middle C is C4. Picking one plays it, with the do above it.</p>
<div class="your-do" id="your-do"></div>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Half steps and whole steps</h2>
<p>The smallest distance in Western music is the <b>half step</b>, or <b>semitone</b>: from one key on a piano to the very next one, black or white,
or from one fret on a guitar to the next. Two half steps make a <b>whole step</b>, or <b>tone</b>.</p>
<p>The black keys are the half steps in between. Each one is named after its neighbours: the black key just above C is <b>C♯</b>
(C sharp, a half step up from C), and the same key is <b>D♭</b> (D flat, a half step down from D) — one key, two names.
So C to D is a whole step that passes over a black key: C to C♯ is one half step, C♯ to D the other.</p>
<div class="keyboard-wrap keys" data-do="C"></div>
<p>Most white keys have a black key between them, so they are a whole step apart. Two pairs don't: E to F and B to C are only a half step apart,
because there is no key between them. Sung from C, those are mi to fa and ti to do — the two places where the scale takes a small step.
That is why the scale from C needs only white keys: its two half steps land exactly where the white keys are already a half step apart.</p>
<p>Start do anywhere else and the same steps need black keys. From D, a whole step up from E has to skip F and land on the black key <b>F♯</b>,
and the whole step up from B lands on <b>C♯</b>, so that ti is still a half step below high do:</p>
<div class="keyboard-wrap keys" data-do="D"></div>
<p>The keys change, but the distances don’t — whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. That is why do re mi sounds the same from any do,
and why the exercises below work wherever you put yours.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>The major scale is a staircase</h2>
<p>Draw each note as high as it sounds and the scale becomes a staircase with two short stairs in it. Going up from do the steps are
<b>whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half</b> (W W H W W W H). Every major scale has exactly this shape; only the starting note changes.</p>
<div class="staircase-wrap" id="staircase"></div>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>How far from do</h2>
<p>Add up the stairs and you get the distance from do to every note of the scale. The names on the right are what musicians call each distance;
the songs are a quick way to remember how it sounds, if you know them: the jump comes at the words in brackets, or right at the start.</p>
<div class="scroller">
  <table class="from-do" id="from-do"
    data-song2="“Happy Birthday” (“-py Birth-”)"
    data-song3="“When the Saints Go Marching In” (“Oh when”)"
    data-song4="“Here Comes the Bride”"
    data-song5="“Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star” (“-kle, twin-”)"
    data-song6="“My Bonnie Lies over the Ocean” (“My Bon-”)"
    data-song7="“Over the Rainbow”, from “Some-” to “o-” (up the octave, then down a half step)"
    data-song8="“Over the Rainbow” (“Some-where”)"></table>
</div>
<p>Notice the gaps: there is nothing 1, 3, 6, 8 or 10 semitones from do in the scale. Those distances appear between other pairs of notes instead —
mi up to fa is 1, re up to fa is 3, fa up to ti is 6.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Sing the distances</h2>
<p>Each exercise plays a pattern on piano and loops until you stop it. The ladder beside it has a rung for every semitone of the octave, so a jump
of four semitones looks twice as long as a jump of two; the arrow shows the jump you are hearing and its size.</p>
<ol class="notes">
  <li>Listen once. Then play it again and sing along on the syllables — do, re, do, mi — not on “la” or a hum: the syllables are what tie the sound to its place in the scale.</li>
  <li>Watch the ladder while you sing, and say the size of each jump to yourself. Over time the number and the feeling become one thing.</li>
  <li>Tick <b>Gap to sing it back</b>: the piano plays a pair, then leaves the same time for you to sing it alone.</li>
  <li>Slow it down whenever a jump goes out of tune. Accuracy first; speed comes by itself.</li>
</ol>

{{> interval-exercise name="Walk the scale" note="Up one stair at a time, then down. Feel how short mi–fa and ti–do are." pattern="1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1" group=8 tempo=80}}
{{> interval-exercise name="Leaps up from do" note="Always back to do, then a little further each time: up 2, up 4, up 5, up 7, up 9, up 11, up 12." pattern="1 2 1 3 1 4 1 5 1 6 1 7 1 8" tempo=80}}
{{> interval-exercise name="Leaps down from high do" note="The same distances, falling: from high do down 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10 and 12 semitones." pattern="8 7 8 6 8 5 8 4 8 3 8 2 8 1" tempo=80}}
