<h2>Ear check</h2>
<p>The other way round: hear two notes and name the second one. The first is always do (or high do, going down), played from your do;
when you get it, the answer shows how far apart the two notes are.</p>
<div class="ear-check" id="ear-check"></div>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Practise it</h2>
<p>A few minutes a day, every day, does more than a long session once a week. Work down the list; each step builds on the one before.</p>
<ol class="notes">
  <li>Sing “Walk the scale” with the piano until mi–fa and ti–do sound obviously smaller than the other steps.</li>
  <li>Take one leap exercise a day. Sing it with the piano, then with the gap, then try the first pair without any help and check yourself against the piano.</li>
  <li>Away from the page, sing do and then any note of the scale — say do up to la — and count the semitones on your fingers while you hold it.</li>
  <li>Do the ear check going up until you get most right first time, then switch to going down.</li>
  <li>Test yourself without looking: how many semitones is do up to sol? Re up to fa? Ti up to do? Which two steps of the major scale are half steps?
    <details><summary>Answers</summary>7 · 3 · 1 · mi–fa and ti–do.</details></li>
</ol>

<h2>Worth remembering</h2>
<ul class="notes">
  <li>A half step (semitone) is the next key on a piano or the next fret on a guitar; a whole step is two.</li>
  <li>The major scale goes whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half — the half steps are mi–fa and ti–do.</li>
  <li>From do: re 2, mi 4, fa 5, sol 7, la 9, ti 11, do 12 semitones.</li>
  <li>Thirds come in two sizes: 4 semitones (major) and 3 (minor). That one semitone is the difference between a major and a minor chord.</li>
</ul>

